    pr_ref:  # 3bc7
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-30 by measurement 0010 (ADR-0049 open work step 3). This is the\nBLOCKER on retagging the deferred-crossing HANDLE shape, and it is a positive\npiece of work rather than a prohibition.\n\nTHE OBSERVATION. Go's ENTIRE catalogued stdin surface is three rows:\n\n  ipc_recv  os.Stdin            (attribute)\n  ipc_recv  bufio.NewScanner    (function)\n  ipc_recv  bufio.NewReader     (function)\n\nAll three are ADR-0049 deferred crossings -- each returns a handle, not a byte.\nThere is NO row for bufio.Reader.ReadString / ReadBytes / ReadLine, none for\nbufio.Scanner.Scan / Text / Bytes, none for os.File.Read / io.ReadAll. So the\ncall that actually transfers is catalogued nowhere, and the three constructor\nrows are the only representation the crossing has.\n\nWHY THAT BLOCKS THE RETAG, on ADR-0049's own terms. Ruling 3 licenses a retag\nonly against a REPRESENTED crossing, run not reasoned, and cites WI-lunav: a\nrow that \"transmits nothing\", whose every real counterpart is already\ncatalogued, was still load-bearing, because the true path ran through an\nuncatalogued combinator. Here the counterparts are not merely uncatalogued in\npractice -- they are absent from the file. Retagging the three would take Go's\nstdin ingress to ZERO rather than relocating it.\n\nAND THE ROWS ARE EARNING THEIR KEEP. 0010 adjudicated 22 READ-IN-SCOPE\nsituations: 14 true positives, 11 of them USEFUL under ADR-0046, including the\nthree CONFIGURED-ACTION findings in caddy that reach ADR-0046's own motivating\nexample. The mechanism is that the handle is consumed in the scope that built\nit -- `reader := bufio.NewReader(os.Stdin)` at line 26 and\n`readLineWithContext(ctx, reader, os.Stdin)` at line 129 of the same function\n-- so \"the scope this call does not name\" IS the caller's, and minting at the\nconstructor is a sound over-approximation rather than a mis-attribution.\n\nTHE WORK. Catalogue the transfer calls, per language, so the crossing has\nsomewhere to live:\n\n  go      bufio.Reader.{ReadString,ReadBytes,ReadLine,ReadRune,ReadSlice}\n          bufio.Scanner.{Scan,Text,Bytes}\n          os.File.Read, io.ReadAll   (io.ReadAll(os.Stdin) is the caddy idiom)\n  python  sys.stdin.{read,readline,readlines}, io.TextIOWrapper.read*\n  java    java.io.BufferedReader.readLine, InputStream.read\n  c/cpp   fgets/fread on stdin, std::getline\n  rust    std::io::Stdin.{read_line,lines,read_to_string}\n\nThen, and only then, re-run 0010's arm 2 and check the 11 useful findings\nsurvive the retag of the constructors. If they do, the constructors may move.\nIf they do not, they stay and the record says why.\n\nA SECOND DEFECT IS ADJACENT AND FILED SEPARATELY (see the sibling item): even\nbefore the retag question, `bufio.NewScanner` is declared ipc_recv\nunconditionally while in the 0010 corpus it wraps a FILE or an in-memory buffer\nfar more often than stdin -- 10 of arm 2's 24 true positives are vacuous for\nthat reason and not for ADR-0049's.\n\nREPRO / EVIDENCE\n  docs/measurements/0010-deferred-crossing-findings-per-shape.md\n  ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/step3_adjudication_08302026/arm2_ledger.json"  # 3bc7
    fields: {}  # 3bc7
- op: discuss  # 96d8
  at: '2026-08-30T08:40:57Z'  # 96d8
  by: agent  # 96d8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 96d8
  clock: 2  # 96d8
  nonce: 96d8  # 96d8
  message: "THE BLOCKER THIS ITEM NAMES IS REAL BUT IT IS NOT THE ONE THAT WAS BINDING, and\nthe item would have been done wrong on its own instructions. Measured before any\nrow was written, six receiver shapes, one fixture:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vutav_reads_08302026/.\n\n  reader := bufio.NewReader(os.Stdin)             -> var_type \"Reader\"        DEAD\n  var reader *bufio.Reader = bufio.NewReader(..)  -> var_type \"Reader\"        DEAD\n  var reader *bufio.Reader; reader = ...          -> var_type \"bufio.Reader\"  live\n  func f(reader *bufio.Reader)                    -> var_type \"bufio.Reader\"  live\n\nA bare \"Reader\" resolves to no package, so `reader.ReadString('\\n')` emitted\n`go:external:0-0:ReadString:unresolved`. A catalogued bufio.Reader.ReadString row\nwould have been PRESENT AND DEAD at the two spellings anybody writes -- exactly\nthe presence-is-not-reachability trap measurement 0009 exists to catch. The\ncatalogue arm was blocked on the ANALYZER arm, not the other way round.\n\nROOT CAUSE, and it is WI-doluf's defect in the sibling branch of the same\nfunction. `_type_from_rhs` names a type from the RHS two ways. The\ncomposite_literal branch was fixed to keep the package qualifier\n(&http.Client{} -> http.Client) for precisely this reason. The NewXxx()\nconstructor branch still dropped it, and `_type_identifier_from_node`'s own\ndocstring states the contract it was violating: the full qualified name \"is\ncritical for IO boundary detection\". FIXED: pkg.NewFoo() -> pkg.Foo, with\na.b.NewFoo() abstaining (that operand is a value, so the call is a method and\nthere is no import alias for \"a.b\"). 11 new tests in\ntest_go_constructor_qualifier.py; 5043 mainstream tests green. After the fix all\nfour spellings give bufio.Reader and the read call lands in the `bufio` slot.\n\nTHREE CORRECTIONS TO THIS ITEM'S OWN TEXT.\n\n1. THE NUMBERS ARE PASS-1 FOSSILS. This item says \"14 true positives, 11 of them\n   USEFUL ... including the three CONFIGURED-ACTION findings in caddy\". Published\n   0010 says READ-IN-SCOPE is 7 TP of 22, 7 useful, and CONFIGURED-ACTION is\n   ZERO -- the caddy claim was withdrawn when pass 2 found clause 3 fails. The\n   item was filed from the pre-correction ledger.\n\n2. THE PYTHON ARM IS ALREADY DONE. python.yaml already carries\n   ipc_recv sys.stdin.{read,readline,readlines}, and they FIRE: `sys.stdin.read()`\n   classifies ipc_recv/sys.stdin.read on a live run. So does `f.read()` after\n   `f = open(p)`, via the synthesized `file` type. No python work is owed here.\n\n3. os.File.Read IS STILL BLOCKED, and by WI-lalot rather than by this fix.\n   `os.Open` is not a NewXxx name, so no constructor heuristic reaches it; the\n   handle types only through a declared parameter. That needs the producing-\n   function -> returned-type table, which is WI-lalot's whole subject.\n\nWHAT MUST NOT BE CATALOGUED, on a ruling that already exists. bufio.Reader.\nReadString / Scanner.Scan / Scanner.Text carry the SAME argument-conditioned\nboundary as the constructors, one hop later: what the read crosses is whatever\nthe handle wraps. INV-zumin (satisfied, P1) already partitioned this exact\npopulation and put it in class (b), \"undecidable at the call site ... the honest\nfix is fd-type inference, not row reordering\", and recorded that multiplying such\nrows is \"actively wrong ... the expensive direction\". So the read rows this item\nlists for bufio are NOT addable at a fixed boundary, and adding them would move\nINV-bagok one hop rather than resolve it.\n\nWHAT IS ADDABLE, AND IT IS A BIGGER FAMILY THAN THIS ITEM'S GO SCOPE. The reads\nthat are UNCONDITIONALLY stdin need no argument and no receiver typing, and they\nare missing or misclassified across five languages. Swept by mechanism:\n\n  go       fmt.{Scan,Scanln,Scanf}          ABSENT ENTIRELY\n  python   builtins.input                   ABSENT (and ambiguous_names asserts\n                                            \"not IO boundary\", which is wrong)\n  c        stdio.{getchar,scanf}            declared fs_read\n  haskell  Prelude.{getContents,readLn,interact}  declared fs_read\n  scala    scala.io.StdIn.{readLine,readInt,...}  declared fs_read, with the\n                                            note \"Scala stdlib stdin reading\"\n\nDIRECTION, and it is the expensive one: fs_read is NOT in AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP,\nso every one of those sites mints NO taint source at all. `scanf(\"%s\", buf);\nsystem(buf);` in C produces nothing. cpp's std.cin (ipc_recv) and rust's\nstd::io::Stdin.read_line (ipc_recv) are the positive controls -- same family,\nsame shape, classified correctly -- so this is drift, not an unmade decision.\n\nFiled separately rather than folded in, so this item stays the analyzer fix it\nturned out to be."  # 96d8
